ve  volume one and you see we're taking  snapshots at T one through T for each of  those snapshot  is point in time but it's also  incremental so you're only storing the  additional data that you need from the  previous snapshot now on the right you  see this user is taking a copy of disk -  oh sorry  copy a volume one at time - and then  also doing disk cloning at the bottom  making three copies of volume one at  time four potentially for use across  three different machines couple of best  practices to go through this morning one  thing I didn't mention earlier but you  might have read on this slide is that  persistent disk gives you maximum  performance without the need for a raid  or striping across multiple volumes  persistent disk performance is  exclusively tied to the disk size so we  give you an allocation of i/o per  gigabyte and bandwidth per gigabyte that  increases linearly up to the bandwidth  cap sorry up up to the caps so unlike  other platforms you may have seen  there's no additional need to add add  volumes for performance also we really  suggest you look at both your i/o size  and cue depths so for high ops workloads  we suggest 16 kilobyte block sizes are  smaller and larger io q depths which  really help performance at those lower  block sizes specifically for high i/o  we suggest a queue depth of one for  every 400 800 I ops now  on the other side on high throughput  workloads we suggest block sizes of 256  K or larger and enabling the read ahead  cache in the OS finally we really  suggest aligning your i/o to 4 K  boundaries what you see on the right is  an example of 6 k writes at the top how  persistent disk sees this in the middle  and then how it's actually written to  the volume on the bottom and what's a  little less apparent is that for every 6  K right you actually up to touch 2  blocks  so you're effectively doing 30% more  disk access for the same amount of  traffic and where if you just aligned  your rights to 4k boundaries you can